    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Joint zones occur as planar subparallel arrays of closely spaced joints, and in echelon arrays. Strain localization produced by shearing of these zones results in progressive fragmentation which eventually leads to formation of gouge cored faults. Fragmentation resulting from shear occurs at irregularities in the joint traces, at joint intersections, and in the span between overlapping adjacent joint~. Fragmentation loci are heterogenously distributed during early stages of fault evolution. As slip accumulates, a through-going damaged zone is formed within which grain size is reduced. Field mapping indicates that deformation can occur as a result of shear, dilation, or contraction within sheared echelon joint zones. The style of deformation is related to the original joint zone geometry.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
